            <div xml:id="d84">

                <pb facs="0159" n="993" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg993"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 658.119/561</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Page</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-08-27T19:00:00-04:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">August 27, 1918, 7 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1008. For Sheldon [from
                    War Trade Board]:</p>
                <p>No. 1094. Your 1217, Embassy 1024, August 12, 7 p.m, We note procedure proposed
                    by Contraband Committee relative to imports into Sweden from South America and
                    that Contraband Committee approval will be cabled to British consul at port of
                    shipment, who will certify correctness of manifests. We agree with your
                    suggestion in so far as it implies that the Contraband Committee, which is an
                    inter-Allied organization, should not utilize exclusively British consular
                    service. We hesitate, however, to require a mere duplication of work through
                    issuance of certificate by American consul similar to that to be issued by
                    British consul. Would it be feasible to have a joint certificate issued or
                    perhaps to have a territorial division so that in certain countries British
                    consul will issue certificates and in other countries American consul. Such a
                    procedure might, of course, involve objections on the part of French and Italian
                    Governments. We attribute considerable importance to the local prestige which
                    will accrue from the issuance of the consul’s certificate and we would like your
                    further suggestions, after considering our viewpoint as above expressed.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
